# standup - environment template.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in the values. .env is gitignored; this file is not,
# so it must never contain a real secret.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# jac.toml reads these with ${VAR} interpolation, so the names must match.
#
# Interpolation reads the PROCESS environment, not this file, so export it
# before starting the server or every ${VAR} ships through as a literal:
#
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#   jac start main.jac

# --- Where the app is reachable ---------------------------------------------
# Origin of this deployment, INCLUDING the scheme and any port. It becomes the
# SSO host and the /auth/callback URL, so a missing scheme breaks the sign-in
# round trip. Leave empty for local work and jac.toml falls back to
# http://localhost:8000.
#   e.g. HOST=https://standup.example.com
HOST=

# --- AI assistant (byLLM) ---------------------------------------------------
# Used by the v3 assistant. byLLM reads the provider key from the environment.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- Single sign-on (optional) ----------------------------------------------
# Leave a pair empty to hide that provider's button on the auth page.
# Google: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# GitHub: https://github.com/settings/developers
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- GitHub integration (optional) ------------------------------------------
# A GitHub App, separate from the SSO pair above. SSO only proves who someone
# is; the runtime discards that OAuth token, so reading issues and pull
# requests needs its own credential.
#
# Create one at https://github.com/settings/apps/new
#   Callback URL:      <HOST>/workspace?tab=github   (the Workspace GitHub tab)
#   Setup URL:         <HOST>/workspace?tab=github   and tick "Redirect on update"
#   Request user authorization (OAuth) during installation:  ON
#       (this is what proves the installer owns the installation; without it
#        the connect step refuses, because an installation id alone is
#        guessable)
#   Webhooks:          OFF (standup pulls on demand, nothing is pushed to it)
#   Repository permissions: Issues = Read and write, Pull requests = Read,
#                           Metadata = Read
# Then generate a private key, and leave every field below empty to hide the
# GitHub tab entirely.
GITHUB_APP_ID=
# The URL slug of the app, from https://github.com/apps/<slug>
GITHUB_APP_SLUG=
# From the app's own OAuth credentials, NOT the SSO pair above.
GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The generated .pem. Either base64 it into one line (recommended):
#   GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=$(base64 -i your-app.private-key.pem | tr -d '\n')
# or paste the PEM with literal \n escapes between the lines.
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=

# --- Monitoring (optional) --------------------------------------------------
# Only needed when deploying the Kubernetes Grafana stack; the /metrics
# endpoint itself needs nothing here. Set it rather than accepting the
# built-in default, which is a well-known password.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- Relay (derived work state) ---------------------------------------------
# Relay reads a repo and derives who every thread is waiting on. It is
# READ-ONLY: no walker in walkers/relay.jac ever calls POST, so a token with
# read-only Issues + Pull requests + Metadata is all it needs (and all it
# should be given).
#
# A fine-grained token scoped to just the repo below is the safe choice; a
# classic token with `repo` grants WRITE and should not be used here.
GITHUB_TOKEN=
# "owner/name" of the repo Relay derives from.
RELAY_REPO=
# GitHub login of whoever runs this workspace. Only the personal view (/you)
# uses it, and the on-page picker overrides it, so it is optional.
RELAY_ME=

# Clocks are POLICY, in hours: how long it is acceptable for work to sit in
# one pair of hands before it is surfaced. Tuned rarely. Defaults are
# build 24, judge 48, validate 72.
RELAY_CLOCK_BUILD=
RELAY_CLOCK_JUDGE=
RELAY_CLOCK_VALIDATE=
